WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.480
Look at a map of the world and trace your

2
00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.960
finger to the western edge of Australia.

3
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.320
You'll find Perth, an incredibly isolated
city.

4
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.400
Meanwhile, an electronic music revolution
was detonating across the northern

5
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.120
hemisphere, surging out of warehouses in
London, Berlin, and Detroit.

6
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.320
But consider the technological reality of
this era.

7
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.840
There was no broadband internet, no
streaming services.

8
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.560
Digital music catalogs did not exist.

9
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.600
If you were a teenager in a suburban
Australian bedroom

10
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.600
craving this new, aggressive sound, you
faced an immense logistical

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.440
hurdle. How do you import an underground
cultural movement across

12
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.880
thousands of miles of ocean without any
digital infrastructure to

13
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.760
carry it? You couldn't just download the
culture.

14
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.280
Music had to be treated as physical cargo,
turning consumption

15
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.680
into a high-stakes logistical challenge.

16
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.120
The entire output of the global electronic
underground was pressed

17
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.240
almost exclusively onto vinyl records,
which had to be physically

18
00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.760
shipped to Australia.

19
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.160
By the time these obscure imports arrived,
they were extremely

20
00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.280
rare and far too expensive for most locals
to buy.

21
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.480
For the vast majority of listeners, the FM
radio dial

22
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.440
became the single fragile lifeline to hear
them.

23
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:15.080
The broadcast tower for Perth's community
station, RTR-FM, operated as

24
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.640
an exclusive physical bridge, connecting
isolated suburban listeners directly to

25
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.880
the global electronic underground.

26
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.600
Within the station, dance music had gained
a foothold through

27
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.440
daytime programming. A show called Full
Frequency provided a measure

28
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.200
of institutional legitimacy, translating
the energy of club culture for

29
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.040
a broader daytime audience.

30
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.240
But the fringes of the genre required a
different environment.

31
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.000
That space was claimed by Auditory Spiral,
a specialized broadcast

32
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.880
designed by DJs Seamus and Mikawai as a
deep, dark

33
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:46.360
alternative to the club-friendly sounds of
the day.

34
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:51.880
It operated under extreme chronological
constraints, broadcasting strictly from midnight

35
00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.720
on Sunday through to 6 a.m.

36
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:54.760
on Monday morning.

37
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.320
That uninterrupted six-hour window was
crucial.

38
00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.000
Without the commercial pressure of
frequent interjections or rapid tempo

39
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.200
shifts, presenters could engage in extreme
long-form mixing, letting 12-minute

40
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.480
tracks of minimal techno and dark ambient
unfold at their

41
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.040
natural pace. This extended duration
transformed the broadcast into a

42
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.600
nocturnal laboratory, pushing the most
challenging fringes of electronic music

43
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.120
into the Perth suburbs.

44
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.120
Pulling off this weekly broadcast required
navigating a major physical

45
00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.920
friction. RTR-FM didn't actually own any
permanent DJ equipment.

46
00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.160
Every Sunday night, the presenters,
Seamus, Nikolai, and Roger, had

47
00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.240
to physically haul their personal
turntables, mixing desks, and heavy

48
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.560
crates of records across the city in the
middle of

49
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.440
the night. They would arrive at the
station and construct

50
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.840
a temporary makeshift club setup directly
inside the radio booth.

51
00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.400
From there, it required the highly
specialized skill of manually

52
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.080
beat matching and live mixing obscure
white label imports and

53
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.320
exclusive dub plates continuously for six
straight hours.

54
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.120
When the sun finally came up on Monday
morning, they

55
00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.760
had to break the entire rig down, pack it
away,

56
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.000
and clear out for the daytime hosts.

57
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.400
This grueling, unglamorous weekly labor
served as the mechanical engine

58
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.920
that sustained the underground electronic
music scene in Perth.

59
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.520
Because the tracks hitting the airwaves
were extremely limited European

60
00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:22.200
pressings, listeners couldn't simply go
out and buy the albums.

61
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.080
Instead, they sat up late and taped the
music directly

62
00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.680
off the radio waves.

63
00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.040
These home recordings birthed a thriving
secondary market.

64
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.600
Cassettes were meticulously dubbed,
hand-labeled, and traded within the community.

65
00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:39.880
Bypassing traditional distribution
networks, these physical artifacts circulated through high

66
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.440
school hallways, independent record shops,
and subversive clothing outlets.

67
00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.960
Through these traded magnetic tapes, an
ephemeral overnight radio broadcast

68
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.440
was converted into a permanent open-source
audio syllabus, educating a

69
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.160
generation of future DJs and producers.

70
00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.600
The tangible result of this audio
education was a rapidly

71
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.320
expanding local ecosystem of illicit
warehouse raves and improvised venues.

72
00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.240
This culture manifested in events like the
famously surreal Star

73
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.680
Wars-themed parties held seven floors
underground in a Perth car

74
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.840
park. Auditory Spiral operated as the
crucial Sunday night anchor

75
00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.200
within a wider matrix of nocturnal
programming, linking up with

76
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.320
shows like Beats Per Minute and Rhythm
Tripping to collectively

77
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.680
support this movement.

78
00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.600
By bridging the gap between European
studios and Perth's suburban

79
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.920
bedrooms, this weekly broadcast provided
the cultural oxygen for an

80
00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.160
isolated scene to survive and build its
own history.

